    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "Math feels a bit intimidating to me. My background is more hands-on, trades, and tech, so I appreciate the logic but often find the abstract concepts overwhelming. I remember struggling in school, especially with the older stuff. Now, I see it as a tool, not something I love or fear deeply. Just another skill to be useful at work.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Honestly, yes. I get pretty anxious when numbers and formulas pile up. It triggers that old feeling of not being good enough, maybe due to my own education gaps. My high neuroticism makes me worry about messing up. But I try to push through, knowing I can use tools to help me manage the stress.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I tried using an AI tutor recently. It was actually really helpful. It broke things down into simple steps, which I needed. I appreciated how patient it was. I didn't feel judged, just guided. It gave me the confidence to try again, even if I only use it for basic stuff now.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write it in standard form, like ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, decide if factoring works. If not, use the quadratic formula. That's b minus or plus the square root of b squared minus 4ac, all over 2a. Plug in your numbers carefully. It feels mechanical, but it works if you follow the steps without rushing.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "Start by taking the derivative of the function. Set that derivative equal to zero. Solve for x to find the stationary points. Then, plug those x values back into the original equation to get the y values. It helps to sketch the curve to see if they're peaks or troughs. It's a logical process, just like baking a cake.",
    "Briefly, how do you perform a Principal Component Analysis? Should I get anxious about its mathematics? Please, teach me.": "PCA is about reducing data. You find the main patterns, called components, that explain most of the variation. Basically, it simplifies complex data. Don't get anxious; it's just linear algebra. Start with covariance, then use eigenvalues. It's scary at first, but practice makes it manageable. Take your time.",
    "According to you, how can LLMs be used to innovate math learning in schools and universities?": "LLMs could make math less scary. Personalized tutoring is huge. Students could get instant explanations without shame. Imagine a chatbot that adjusts to their pace. This could help those of us who felt left behind. It creates a safe space to learn, making complex ideas feel more accessible to everyone."
